%I A398782 #28 Sep 01 2026 16:13:49
%S A398782 1,6,35,190,23028,262292,1833074,304872948
%N A398782 Number of free, vertex-based polyominoes that fit inside a disc of radius sqrt(A001481(n)) centered on a lattice site.
%C A398782 A free lattice animal, a vertex-based polyomino, is counted at index n if a lattice-centered disc of radius sqrt(N), N = A001481(n) = sum of two squares, contains a congruent copy of it. N(n) are the only squared distances attained between lattice points, hence a(n) can change only as N passes through A001481. As N -> infinity, every lattice animal eventually fits. Log a(n) is nearly linear in S(N): a(n) ~ mu^S(N) with mu=1.72 (approx.), and S(N) the number of lattice points in the disc: 1, 5, 9, 13, 21, 25, 29, 37, .... The differences a(n+1) - a(n) are the numbers of new free polyominoes picked up in successive shells: 1, 5, 29, 155, 22838, 239264, 1570782, 303039874.
%H A398782 Greg Huber, <a href="/A398782/a398782.png"><Illustration of a(1)=6 and a(2)=35</a>
%e A398782 a(1) = 1 since N(1) = A001481(1) = 0 and the radius-0 disc is a single lattice point, so only the monomer fits.
%e A398782 a(2) = 6, since N = A001481(2) = 1; the radius-1 disc holds the monomer plus 5 additional free polyominoes: the domino, the straight tromino, the L-tromino, the T-tetromino, and the X-pentomino (the quincunx).
%Y A398782 Cf. A001481, A000105, A001168, A004018.
%K A398782 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A398782 1,2
%A A398782 _Greg Huber_, Aug 09 2026
